#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3758cf02-ba4b-539a-bc61-8c2030ff6f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54b95013-9673-571d-a385-1ada2dbd7596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54b95013-9673-571d-a385-1ada2dbd7596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290994} :answer-field/id #uuid "d77c3cd4-4822-5b88-b531-15ff6b1e643c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292391} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abb9bd8e-4e36-5095-84b1-7e4d92640618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abb9bd8e-4e36-5095-84b1-7e4d92640618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291378} :answer-field/id #uuid "e319a330-863d-5a2b-83b4-4b5600e073c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77ac52fb-b13e-5bb0-9619-5d3b00902a0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-264778" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x+3}-7x\\cdot {2}^{x+3}+3\\cdot {2}^{x+3}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x+3}-7x\\cdot {2}^{x+3}+3\\cdot {2}^{x+3} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-7x+3){2}^{x+3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-7x+3=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{2}^{x+3}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}-7x+3=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2x-1)(x-3)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=\\frac{1}{2},3.$\n\n- The equation ${2}^{x+3}=0$ has no solutions because ${2}^{x+3}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292664 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8aaf12cf-8596-5960-bc68-5ca3eb5fab0f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e00ad2a-0346-5764-8bd0-cbcc0f4a797a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e00ad2a-0346-5764-8bd0-cbcc0f4a797a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291167} :answer-field/id #uuid "4304fe74-f9d5-5270-981d-8bec5b17751e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291954} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c61ad1b3-442b-57c6-94cb-530214be2c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c61ad1b3-442b-57c6-94cb-530214be2c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291485} :answer-field/id #uuid "db84ff3f-88d9-57d9-80ab-3958548a8d86" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4930566b-e427-5630-b850-4beaebc0bb52" :question/math-academy-id "q-264787" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $4{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-4}=144\\cdot {5}^{x-4}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-4} & =144\\cdot {5}^{x-4} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-4}-144\\cdot {5}^{x-4} & =0 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}-144)\\cdot {5}^{x-4} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-144=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{x-4}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $4{x}^{2}-144=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}-36) & =0 \\\\ 4(x+6)(x-6) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-6,6.$\n\n- The equation $4=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${5}^{x-4}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{x-4}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-6\\,\\text{and}\\,x=6.\n$$"}]}
